### 5. Justification of Design Choices

| Decision | Reasoning |
|----------|-----------|
| **Sequential overall flow (G1 runtime `;`)** | Food delivery is a pipeline: obtain → transport → deliver → dish handling. Each stage depends on the previous. |
| **Fallback for delivery (G4)** | The robot prefers to hand the meal directly to the patient. If the patient cannot retrieve, it falls back to placing the meal on the table. |
| **Fallback for door opening (G6)** | The robot first tries to open the door itself. If that fails (e.g., door locked), it asks a human, and if still impossible, it coordinates with another robot. |
| **Fallback for cooperation (G7)** | The robot first tries to cooperate with the patient. If the patient cannot retrieve, it asks a companion or nurse. If none available, it cooperates with another robot. |
| **Parallel picking up meals (G2.1 runtime `#`)** | The robot can pick up several meals at once, so these actions can be performed concurrently. |
| **Parallel indication and assistance (G4.3 & G4.4)** | Indicating the meal number and assisting retrieval can happen simultaneously with the chosen delivery action. |
| **Query goal for patient ability (G8)** | The robot needs to know whether the patient can retrieve the meal; this is purely informational and does not trigger an action. |
| **Perform goals for all actions** | All tasks are concrete actions that the robot must execute; they are not purely informational. |
| **Number of robots for door opening & cooperation** | Unassisted door opening and patient assistance require one robot. Human assistance also requires one robot. Door opening with another robot and cooperation with another robot require two robots. |
| **Task locations** | Tasks are located in the kitchen, corridor, room, or internal memory, matching the physical or logical context of the action. |

